{"abstract":"Sterol transport proteins (STPs) play a pivotal role in cholesterol homeostasis and therefore are essential for healthy human physiology. Despite recent advances in dissecting functions of STPs in the human cell, there is still a significant knowledge gap regarding their specific biological functions and a lack of suitable selective probes for their study. Here, we profile fluorescent steroid-based probes across ten STPs, uncovering substantial differences in their selectivity, aiding the retrospective and prospective interpretation of biological results generated with those probes. These results guided the establishment of an STP screening panel combining diverse biophysical assays, enabling the evaluation of 42 steroid-based natural products and derivatives. Combining this with a thorough structural analysis revealed the molecular basis for STP-specific selectivity profiles, leading to the uncovering of several new potent and selective Aster-B inhibitors and supporting the role of this protein in steroidogenesis.","author_count":9,"contributors":["Depta, Laura","Bryce-Rogers, Hogan P.","Dekker, Nienke J.","Bønke, Anna Wiehl","Camporese, Nicolò","Qian, Mingxing","Xu, Yuanjian","Covey, Douglas F.","Laraia, Luca"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-818079"],"filters":{"added":"2025-08","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, North","OECD"],"corresponding_author":["Laraia, Luca"],"country":["United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Washington University St. Louis"],"keyword":["Natural products","Small molecules","Structure Activity Relationship"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-DTU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-DTU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"endogenous and fluorescent sterols reveal the molecular basis for ligand selectivity of human sterol transporters","src":["dtu"],"src-code":["DI-U-DTU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jlr.2024.100738","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-818079","identifier":[{"id":"PMC11830314","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"105000521430","type":"scopus"},{"id":"39746449","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["dtu-d3cb2ce0-e813-403d-8a67-53e6f501e05f"],"keyword":["Ligand selectivity","Natural products","Small molecules","Sterol transport proteins","Structure activity relationship"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-944E65B3C0D23C15437B2782269624DD"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://backend.orbit.dtu.dk/ws/files/405799670/1-s2.0-S0022227524002438-main.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jlr.2024.100738","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"dtu technical university of denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-DTU","l3nl":"Technical University of Denmark","l3nn":"DTU Technical University of Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"DTU Technical University of Denmark","namevar":["Technical University of Denmark, Department of Chemistry","Technical University of Denmark, Department of Chemistry, Organic Chemistry"],"nora_code":"DI-U-DTU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"us","key":"washington university st louis","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"Washington University St. Louis","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Laura","key":"deptapau","last":"Depta","locid":"278d4609-eae1-4a92-8062-ec71112fea84","name":"Depta, Laura","orcid":"0000-0002-4076-7316","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Hogan P.","key":"brycerogerspau","last":"Bryce-Rogers","locid":"ecd2c287-cbb8-4aa0-b930-b3985125261a","name":"Bryce-Rogers, Hogan P.","orcid":"0000-0002-3186-9601","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Nienke J.","key":"dekkerpau","last":"Dekker","locid":"624897f3-90ca-422e-95d7-1dc3f8cf0b27","name":"Dekker, Nienke J.","orcid":"0000-0002-9258-6613","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Anna Wiehl","key":"bønkepau","last":"Bønke","name":"Bønke, Anna Wiehl","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Nicolò","key":"camporesepau","last":"Camporese","locid":"45c57164-a212-4981-8a76-472f1aef8307","name":"Camporese, Nicolò","orcid":"0009-0003-2363-2115","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Mingxing","key":"qianpau","last":"Qian","name":"Qian, Mingxing","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Yuanjian","key":"xupau","last":"Xu","name":"Xu, Yuanjian","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Douglas F.","key":"coveypau","last":"Covey","name":"Covey, Douglas F.","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Luca","key":"laraiapau","last":"Laraia","locid":"3bb2d04a-6f42-4d31-abe1-f116f8733b75","name":"Laraia, Luca","orcid":"0000-0001-6692-3412","rank":9,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["39746449"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1016/j.jlr.2024.100738","issn":["00222275","15397262"],"issn_org":["00222275"],"issue":"2","journal":"Journal of Lipid Research","paperid":"100738","publisher":"American Society for Biochemistry and Molecular Biology Inc.","status":"p","title":"Journal of Lipid Research","volume":"66"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-105000521430"],"sdg":["3. Good Health and Well-being","All UN SDG Classification"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Technical University of Denmark","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["dtu-d3cb2ce0-e813-403d-8a67-53e6f501e05f"],"src":["DTU Technical University of Denmark"],"title":"Endogenous and fluorescent sterols reveal the molecular basis for ligand selectivity of human sterol transporters","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-818079","year":"2025","yearsub":"2025"}
{"abstract":"Despite the widespread use of “artificial intelligence” (AI) framing in Natural Language Processing (NLP) research, it is not clear what researchers mean by “intelligence”. To that end, we present the results of a survey on the notion of “intelligence” among researchers and its role in the research agenda. The survey elicited complete responses from 303 researchers from a variety of fields including NLP, Machine Learning (ML), Cognitive Science, Linguistics, and Neuroscience. We identify 3 criteria of intelligence that the community agrees on the most: generalization, adaptability, & reasoning. Our results suggests that the perception of the current NLP systems as “intelligent” is a minority position (29%). Furthermore, only 16.2% of the respondents see developing intelligent systems as a research goal, and these respondents are more likely to consider the current systems intelligent.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Højer, Bertram","Jakobsen, Terne Sasha Thorn","Rogers, Anna","Heinrich, Stefan"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-818244"],"event":{"acronym":"ACL","title":"Meeting of the Association for Computational Linguistics"},"filters":{"added":"2025-08","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Adaptability","Generalization","Intelligence","Natural Language Processing","Reasoning"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"research community perspectives on intelligence and large language models","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2505.20959","version":"Pre-print"},"id":"S01-000.00-818244","ids":["itu-7b79a87e-93e1-4692-bd39-33e082c20ecd"],"keyword":["adaptability","generalization","intelligence","natural language processing","reasoning"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-5297661960450E9CB6E306059914D9DB"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"rem","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2505.20959","version":"Pre-print"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2505.20959","version":"Pre-print"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Data Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data Science, Machine Learning","IT University of Copenhagen, Data Science, NLPnorth"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"dk","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["University of Copenhagen"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Bertram","key":"højerpau","last":"Højer","locid":"363f336f-ee57-420f-990f-11e8c2f0e2c6","name":"Højer, Bertram","orcid":"0009-0002-2990-8752","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Terne Sasha Thorn","key":"jakobsenpau","last":"Jakobsen","name":"Jakobsen, Terne Sasha Thorn","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Anna","key":"rogerspau","last":"Rogers","locid":"c40e1e8a-50ee-4a7c-b7f0-2fc0fdd62adc","name":"Rogers, Anna","orcid":"0000-0002-4845-4023","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Stefan","key":"heinrichpau","last":"Heinrich","locid":"1abda59f-c493-4824-a7b1-7470d66f08d1","name":"Heinrich, Stefan","orcid":"0000-0001-9913-3206","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2505.20959","place":"Vienna","publisher":"Association for Computational Linguistics","series":"Findings of the Association for Computational Linguistics","status":"p","title":"Findings of the Association for Computational Linguistics 2025"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-7b79a87e-93e1-4692-bd39-33e082c20ecd"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Research Community Perspectives on “Intelligence” and Large Language Models","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-818244","year":"2025","yearsub":"2025"}
{"abstract":"The rapid adoption of AI across diverse domains has led to the development of organisational guidelines that vary significantly, even within the same sector. This paper examines AI policies in two domains, news organisations and universities, to understand how bottom-up governance approaches shape AI usage and oversight. By analysing these policies, we identify key areas of convergence and divergence in how organisations address risks such as bias, privacy, misinformation, and accountability. We then explore the implications of these findings for international AI legislation, particularly the EU AI Act, highlighting gaps where practical policy insights could inform regulatory refinements. Our analysis reveals that organisational policies often address issues such as AI literacy, disclosure practices, and environmental impact, areas that are underdeveloped in existing international frameworks. We argue that lessons from domain-specific AI policies can contribute to more adaptive and effective AI governance at the global level. This study provides actionable recommendations for policymakers seeking to bridge the gap between local AI practices and international regulations.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Kaffee, Lucie-Aimée Frimelle","Rogers, Anna","Atanasova, Pepa"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-818822"],"filters":{"added":"2025-08","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Hugging Face"],"keyword":["cs AI","cs CY"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"und","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"local differences global lessons insights from organisation policies for international legislation","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dw","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2503.05737","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-818822","ids":["ku-3004d1fc-4d5f-4649-b254-be77184c31b8"],"keyword":["cs.AI","cs.CY"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-D884EEBCB65C371F0387DF9916CD1A74"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/454604099/2503.05737v1.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2503.05737","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"hugging face","name":"Hugging Face","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"dk","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"3","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Science, Department of Computer Science, Natural Language Processing"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","first":"Lucie-Aimée Frimelle","key":"kaffeepau","last":"Kaffee","name":"Kaffee, Lucie-Aimée Frimelle","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Anna","key":"rogerspau","last":"Rogers","name":"Rogers, Anna","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","first":"Pepa","key":"atanasovapau","last":"Atanasova","locid":"876ac9d6-217c-4e63-9e65-8f010272dce2","name":"Atanasova, Pepa","orcid":"0000-0002-0023-2616","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2503.05737","pages":"20","publisher":"arXiv preprint","status":"p"},"review":"Undetermined","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-3004d1fc-4d5f-4649-b254-be77184c31b8"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Local Differences, Global Lessons : Insights from Organisation Policies for International Legislation","type":"Working Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-818822","year":"2025","yearsub":"2025"}
{"abstract":"Arctic landscapes occupy a nexus of environmental change processes, globally significant soil carbon stores, wildlife populations, and subsistence-based human societies. In response to rapid climate warming, tundra ecosystems are experiencing widespread changes to vegetation and underlying permafrost, coupled with an array of ecological disturbances that are expected to intensify in the future. Declines in the extent of the cryosphere on land (permafrost and seasonal snow) and offshore (sea-ice) raise the question of whether and for how long warmer portions of the Low Arctic will fit established concepts of “what is Arctic,” given the influence the cryosphere has historically had on tundra ecosystem structure and function. The era of spaceborne observation of circumpolar tundra greenness, in the form of the Normalized Difference Vegetation Index (NDVI), has entered its fifth decade and provides foundational information concerning ecosystem conditions and responses to climatic trends, variability, ecological disturbance, and successional processes. Here we review the evolving story of Arctic greening, and synthesize long-term spaceborne records of NDVI, climatic data, field observations, and the knowledge base of Arctic residents to place the last four decades of Arctic environmental change in context, and establish expectations and research priorities for the coming decade. Greenness dynamics display high spatio-temporal variability, reflecting complex interactions of climatic warming and variability, landscape history, ecological disturbance, and other factors. Nonetheless, long-term increases in NDVI—commonly known as “the greening of the Arctic”—remain prominent across large areas in all available long-term spaceborne datasets and align with long-term shifts in vegetation structure documented in disparate Arctic regions. Common shifts reported from the Low Arctic, such as shrubification, generally portend declines in floristic diversity, and shifts in fauna that favor boreal forest species. Despite lingering uncertainties regarding trend attribution and sources of interannual variability, the sequence of record-high circumpolar tundra greenness values observed since 2020 provides strong evidence that Arctic tundra ecosystems have entered a state without historic precedent on timescales approaching a millennium.","author_count":32,"contributors":["Frost, Gerald V.","Bhatt, Uma S.","Macander, Matthew J.","Berner, Logan T.","Walker, Donald A.","Raynolds, Martha K.","Magnússon, Rúna","Bartsch, Annett","Bjerke, Jarle W.","Epstein, Howard E.","Forbes, Bruce C.","Goetz, Scott J.","Hoy, Elizabeth E.","Karlsen, Stein R.","Kumpula, Timo","Lantz, Trevor C.","Lara, Mark J.","López-Blanco, Efrén","Montesano, Paul M.","Neigh, Christopher S.R.","Nitze, Ingmar","Orndahl, Kathleen M.","Park, Taejin","Phoenix, Gareth K.","Rocha, Adrian V.","Rogers, Brendan M.","Schaepman-Strub, Gabriela","Tømmervik, Hans","Verdonen, Mariana","Veremeeva, Alexandra","Virkkala, Anna Maria","Waigl, Christine F."],"cuid":["S01-000.00-816912"],"filters":{"added":"2025-08","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, North","Europe, EU","Europe, Non-EU","Nordic","OECD"],"corresponding_author":["Frost, Gerald V."],"country":["Canada","Finland","Germany","Iceland","Netherlands","Norway","Switzerland","United Kingdom","United States"],"glorg":["ABR Inc. Environmental Research and Services","Adnet Systems","Agricultural University of Iceland","Alfred Wegener Institute for Polar and Marine Research (Helmholz)","All Global Affiliations","Ames Research Center","Bay Area Environmental Research Institute","ETH Zürich","NASA Goddard Space Flight Center","NOAA","NORCE","Natera, Inc","Northern Arizona University","Norwegian Institute for Nature Research","Sheffield Hallam University","UiT The Arctic University of Norway","University of Alaska Fairbanks","University of Eastern Finland","University of Illinois at Urbana-Champaign","University of Lapland","University of Notre Dame","University of Victoria BC","Virginia Commonwealth University","Wageningen University & Research","Woodwell Climate Research Center"],"keyword":["Arctic","Climate change","Greening","Normalized difference vegetation index","Remote sensing","Tundra"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"the changing face of the arctic four decades of greening and implications for tundra ecosystems","src":["au"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU"],"type":"djr","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.3389/fenvs.2025.1525574","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-816912","identifier":[{"id":"105004653435","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["au-ee8bd8c0-7bc6-441a-b4a9-2ba127c3352b"],"keyword":["Arctic","climate change","greening","normalized difference vegetation index","remote sensing","tundra"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-2-4042BF1CF6FD8FB443504A85CFAA56AC"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.3389/fenvs.2025.1525574","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"us","key":"abr inc environmental research and services","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"ABR Inc. Environmental Research and Services","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"us","key":"university of alaska fairbanks","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"University of Alaska Fairbanks","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"us","key":"northern arizona university","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"Northern Arizona University","used":1},{"affno":"4","country":"nl","key":"wageningen university research","loc":["Netherlands","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Wageningen University & Research","used":1}