echnology","loc":["Japan","Asia, East","OECD"],"name":"Tokyo Institute of Technology","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Anna","key":"rogersped","last":"Rogers","locid":"c40e1e8a-50ee-4a7c-b7f0-2fc0fdd62adc","name":"Rogers, Anna","orcid":"0000-0002-4845-4023","rank":1,"role":"Editor","rolecode":"ped"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Jordan","key":"boydgraberped","last":"Boyd-Graber","name":"Boyd-Graber, Jordan","rank":2,"role":"Editor","rolecode":"ped"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Naoaki","key":"okazakiped","last":"Okazaki","name":"Okazaki, Naoaki","rank":3,"role":"Editor","rolecode":"ped"}],"pub":{"place":"Toronto, Canada","publisher":"Association for Computational Linguistics","status":"p"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-e5e9fd1f-8c6f-448e-8a80-e137fd88ea20"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Proceedings of the 61st Annual Meeting of the Association for Computational Linguistics (Volume 1: Long Papers)","type":"Book","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-695306","year":"2023","yearsub":"2023"}
{"abstract":"Both scientific progress and individual researcher careers depend on the quality of peer review, which in turn depends on paper-reviewer matching. Surprisingly, this problem has been mostly approached as an automated recommendation problem rather than as a matter where different stakeholders (area chairs, reviewers, authors) have accumulated experience worth taking into account. We present the results of the first survey of the NLP community, identifying common issues and perspectives on what factors should be considered by paper-reviewer matching systems. This study contributes actionable recommendations for improving future NLP conferences, and desiderata for interpretable peer review assignments.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Jakobsen, Terne Sasha Thorn","Rogers, Anna"],"cuid":["S02-081.58-725104"],"event":{"end":"2022-07-15","place":"Seattle, us","start":"2022-07-10","title":"Proceedings of the 2022 Conference of the North American Chapter of the Association for Computational Linguistics: Human Language Technologies","year":"2022"},"filters":{"added":"2023-07","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"keyword":["Automated Recommendation Systems","Interpretable Peer Review Assignments","NLP Community Survey","Paper reviewer Matching","Peer review"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci","soc"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"what factors should paper reviewer assignments rely on community perspectives on issues and ideals in conference peer review","src":["itu","ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU","DI-U-KU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85138394730","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S02-081.58-725104","identifier":[{"id":"85138394730","type":"scopus"},{"id":"000859869504069","type":"wos"}],"ids":["itu-c3027b3a-72b6-42d7-b607-6cccfceb1356","ku-1a87fefc-8b01-4a43-98bb-54e8da376bcc"],"keyword":["Automated Recommendation Systems","Interpretable Peer Review Assignments","NLP Community Survey","Paper-reviewer Matching","Peer Review"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology","Social Science"],"noi":["Local-1-2-3918C602DCC132C6EA565AE05BA3F185"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Social Sciences, Faculty of Social Sciences, shared expenditure, Copenhagen Center for Social Data Science (SODAS)","Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Social Sciences, Faculty of Social Sciences, shared expenditure, Social Data Science"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1},{"affno":2,"key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU"}],"person":[{"affno":"1","first":"Terne Sasha Thorn","key":"jakobsenpau","last":"Jakobsen","locid":"551b3a30-c5fb-4e4e-8251-b36deef0f89e","name":"Jakobsen, Terne Sasha Thorn","orcid":"0000-0002-3124-1320","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Terne","key":"thornjakobsenpau","last":"Thorn Jakobsen","name":"Thorn Jakobsen, Terne","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Anna","key":"rogerspau","last":"Rogers","locid":"e8d3de73-eb20-4bda-9f0e-6bf8a7bdac21","name":"Rogers, Anna","orcid":"0000-0002-4845-4023","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"pages":"4810-4823","place":"Seattle, United States","publisher":"Association for Computational Linguistics","status":"p","title":"Proceedings of the 2022 Conference of the North American Chapter of the Association for Computational Linguistics: Human Language Technologies"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","ITU IT University of Copenhagen","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-c3027b3a-72b6-42d7-b607-6cccfceb1356","ku-1a87fefc-8b01-4a43-98bb-54e8da376bcc"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen","KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"What Factors Should Paper-Reviewer Assignments Rely On? Community Perspectives on Issues and Ideals in Conference Peer-Review","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-081.58-725104","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"Two of the most fundamental issues in Natural Language Understanding (NLU) at present are: (a) how it can established whether deep learning-based models score highly on NLU benchmarks for the ”right” reasons; and (b) what those reasons would even be. We investigate the behavior of reading comprehension models with respect to two linguistic ”skills”: coreference resolution and comparison. We propose a definition for the reasoning steps expected from a system that would be ”reading slowly”, and compare that with the behavior of five models of the BERT family of various sizes, observed through saliency scores and counterfactual explanations. We find that for comparison (but not coreference) the systems based on larger encoders are more likely to rely on the ”right” information, but even they struggle with generalization, suggesting that they still learn specific lexical patterns rather than the general principles of comparison.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Ray Choudhury, Sagnik","Rogers, Anna","Augenstein, Isabelle"],"cuid":["S02-091.18-692201"],"event":{"acronym":"COLING","end":"2022-10-17","place":"Gyeongju, Republic of Korea","start":"2022-10-12","title":"THE 29TH<br/>INTERNATIONAL CONFERENCE ON<br/>COMPUTATIONAL LINGUISTICS","year":"2022"},"filters":{"added":"2023-05","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"continent":["America, North","OECD"],"country":["United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","University of Michigan","University of Michigan, Ann Arbor"],"keyword":["Comparison","Coreference Resolution","Deep Learning Models","Natural Language Understanding","Reading comprehension"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"machine reading fast and slow when do models understand language","src":["itu","ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU","DI-U-KU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/341057093/Machine_Reading_Fast_and_Slow.pdf","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S02-091.18-692201","identifier":[{"id":"85139889711","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-05650ee3-30e9-42d4-aec3-4e47cce9ecc2","ku-8fc5f176-b969-4f01-a7f5-fcf8fe8154ee"],"keyword":["Comparison","Coreference Resolution","Deep Learning Models","Natural Language Understanding","Reading Comprehension"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-2-3323D35353F3B332F8F100C424945CA8"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/341057093/Machine_Reading_Fast_and_Slow.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"rem","url":"https://aclanthology.org/2022.coling-1.8/","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Science, Department of Computer Science, Natural Language Processing"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"us","key":"university of michigan ann arbor","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"University of Michigan, Ann Arbor","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1 2","first":"Sagnik","key":"raychoudhurypau","last":"Ray Choudhury","locid":"8856eb6b-5fc0-485e-b62f-c6237a7a87ff","name":"Ray Choudhury, Sagnik","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Anna","key":"rogerspau","last":"Rogers","locid":"e8d3de73-eb20-4bda-9f0e-6bf8a7bdac21","name":"Rogers, Anna","orcid":"0000-0002-4845-4023","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Isabelle","key":"augensteinpau","last":"Augenstein","locid":"929ec255-2eae-4e6d-b826-5e7abd210f06","name":"Augenstein, Isabelle","orcid":"0000-0003-1562-7909","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"pages":"78-93","publisher":"Association for Computational Linguistics (ACL)","status":"p","title":"Proceedings of the 29th International Conference on Computational Linguistics"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-05650ee3-30e9-42d4-aec3-4e47cce9ecc2","ku-8fc5f176-b969-4f01-a7f5-fcf8fe8154ee"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen","KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Machine Reading, Fast and Slow: When Do Models “Understand” Language?","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-091.18-692201","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"BACKGROUND: Recent population-based female breast cancer and prostate cancer polygenic risk scores (PRS) have been developed. We assessed the associations of these PRS with breast and prostate cancer risks for male BRCA1 and BRCA2 pathogenic variant carriers. METHODS: 483 BRCA1 and 1318 BRCA2 European ancestry male carriers were available from the Consortium of Investigators of Modifiers of BRCA1/2 (CIMBA). A 147-single nucleotide polymorphism (SNP) prostate cancer PRS (PRSPC) and a 313-SNP breast cancer PRS were evaluated. There were 3 versions of the breast cancer PRS, optimized to predict overall (PRSBC), estrogen receptor (ER)-negative (PRSER-), or ER-positive (PRSER+) breast cancer risk. RESULTS: PRSER+ yielded the strongest association with breast cancer risk. The odds ratios (ORs) per PRSER+ standard deviation estimates were 1.40 (95% confidence interval [CI] =1.07 to 1.83) for BRCA1 and 1.33 (95% CI = 1.16 to 1.52) for BRCA2 carriers. PRSPC was associated with prostate cancer risk for BRCA1 (OR = 1.73, 95% CI = 1.28 to 2.33) and BRCA2 (OR = 1.60, 95% CI = 1.34 to 1.91) carriers. The estimated breast cancer odds ratios were larger after adjusting for female relative breast cancer family history. By age 85 years, for BRCA2 carriers, the breast cancer risk varied from 7.7% to 18.4% and prostate cancer risk from 34.1% to 87.6% between the 5th and 95th percentiles of the PRS distributions. CONCLUSIONS: Population-based prostate and female breast cancer PRS are associated with a wide range of absolute breast and prostate cancer risks for male BRCA1 and BRCA2 carriers. These findings warrant further investigation aimed at providing personalized cancer risks for male carriers and informing clinical management.","author_count":161,"contributors":["Barnes, Daniel R","Silvestri, Valentina","Leslie, Goska","McGuffog, Lesley","Dennis, Joe","Yang, Xin","Adlard, Julian","Agnarsson, Bjarni A","Ahmed, Munaza","Aittomäki, Kristiina","Andrulis, Irene L","Arason, A