{"abstract":"Marine species may respond and adapt to climate change through shifting spatial distributions, but options may be limited by the occupancy of essential habitats which are anchored in space. Limited knowledge of when spatial constraints are most likely to occur in marine fish life cycles has impeded the development of realistic distribution forecasts. In this study, we develop and implement analytical techniques to identify spatial constraints, defined by both the consistency through which a particular geographic area is used year after year, and by the extent of such area with respect to the entire population range. This approach is applied to simulated data and to ten case-studies including six groundfish species from three subarctic marine systems. Our analyses illustrate that the early phase of the species’ life cycle is more spatially constrained than older life stages. We detected significant species-specific variability in both the degree to which species are anchored in space throughout their life cycle, and the ontogenetic changes in the geographic association. There is an indication that this variability can be explained by the species life history strategy, highlighting the need to extend similar analyses to other species and regions. The presence of ontogenetic spatial constraints, particularly during early life stages, indicates restrictions exist to changes in spatial distribution and questions the assertion that global warming will uniformly result in an increase in abundance and harvest at higher latitudes and decreases at lower latitudes. Our study develops ecological and analytical insights that are critical for accurate projections of species distributions under different climate change scenarios.","author_count":10,"contributors":["Ciannelli, L.","Neuheimer, Anna Beatrice","Stige, L.C.","Frank, K.T.","Durant, J.M.","Hunsicker, M.","Rogers, L.A.","Porter, S.","Ottersen, G.","Yaragina, N.A."],"cuid":["S01-000.00-208824"],"filters":{"added":"2022-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, North","Europe, Non-EU","Nordic","OECD"],"corresponding_author":["Ciannelli, L."],"country":["Canada","Norway","Russia","United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Bedford Institute of Oceanography","NOAA","National Veterinary Institute Norway","Oregon State University","Russian Federal Research Institute of Fisheries and Oceanography","University of Hawaii at Manoa","University of Oslo"],"keyword":["Adaptation","Climate change","Spatial distribution","Spatial ecology","Spawning"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"ontogenetic spatial constraints of sub arctic marine fish species","src":["au"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85117794632","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-208824","identifier":[{"id":"85117794632","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["au-8b57e0d2-a2d3-4338-b19a-d6ee6de2519b"],"keyword":["adaptation","climate change","marine subarctic","spatial distribution","spatial ecology","spawning"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-3-60CBD1E4B34523442A6A1993E67FECC1"],"oai":{"class":"Unused","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2022","yearSub":"2022"},"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"us","key":"oregon state university","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"Oregon State University","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Natural Sciences, Department of Biology, Department of Biology - Aquatic Biology"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"us","key":"university of hawaii at manoa","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"University of Hawaii at Manoa","used":1},{"affno":"4","country":"no","key":"university of oslo","loc":["Norway","Europe, Non-EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"University of Oslo","used":1},{"affno":"5","country":"no","key":"national veterinary institute norway","loc":["Norway","Europe, Non-EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"National Veterinary Institute Norway","used":1},{"affno":"6","country":"ca","key":"bedford institute of oceanography","loc":["Canada","America, North","OECD"],"name":"Bedford Institute of Oceanography","used":1},{"affno":"7","country":"us","key":"noaa","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"NOAA","used":1},{"affno":"8","country":"ru","key":"russian federal research institute of fisheries and oceanography","loc":["Russia","Europe, Non-EU"],"name":"Russian Federal Research Institute of Fisheries and Oceanography","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"L.","key":"ciannellipau","last":"Ciannelli","name":"Ciannelli, L.","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2 3","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Anna Beatrice","key":"neuheimerpau","last":"Neuheimer","locid":"b4aaea34-1dc3-4fb0-8776-7fad99472e16","name":"Neuheimer, Anna Beatrice","orcid":"0000-0002-9470-7140","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"4 5","corresponding":null,"first":"L.C.","key":"stigepau","last":"Stige","name":"Stige, L.C.","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"6","corresponding":null,"first":"K.T.","key":"frankpau","last":"Frank","name":"Frank, K.T.","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"4","corresponding":null,"first":"J.M.","key":"durantpau","last":"Durant","name":"Durant, J.M.","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"7","corresponding":null,"first":"M.","key":"hunsickerpau","last":"Hunsicker","name":"Hunsicker, M.","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"7","corresponding":null,"first":"L.A.","key":"rogerspau","last":"Rogers","name":"Rogers, L.A.","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"7","corresponding":null,"first":"S.","key":"porterpau","last":"Porter","name":"Porter, S.","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"4","corresponding":null,"first":"G.","key":"ottersenpau","last":"Ottersen","name":"Ottersen, G.","rank":9,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"8","corresponding":null,"first":"N.A.","key":"yaraginapau","last":"Yaragina","name":"Yaragina, N.A.","rank":10,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1111/faf.12619","issn":["14672960","14672979"],"issn_org":["14672960"],"issue":"2","journal":"Fish and Fisheries","pages":"342-357","publisher":"Wiley-Blackwell Publishing Ltd","status":"p","title":"Fish and Fisheries","volume":"23"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85117794632"],"search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-8b57e0d2-a2d3-4338-b19a-d6ee6de2519b"],"src":["AU Aarhus University"],"title":"Ontogenetic spatial constraints of sub-arctic marine fish species","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-208824","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"While Transformer-based language models are generally very robust to pruning, there is the recently discovered outlier phenomenon: disabling only 48 out of 110M parameters in BERT-base drops its performance by nearly 30% on MNLI. We replicate the original evidence for the outlier phenomenon and we link it to the geometry of the embedding space. We find that in both BERT and RoBERTa the magnitude of hidden state coefficients corresponding to outlier dimensions correlates with the frequency of encoded tokens in pre-training data, and it also contributes to the “vertical” self-attention pattern enabling the model to focus on the special tokens. This explains the drop in performance from disabling the outliers, and it suggests that to decrease anisotropicity in future models we need pre-training schemas that would better take into account the skewed token distributions.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Puccetti, Giovanni","Rogers, Anna","Drozd, Aleksandr","Dell'Orletta, Felice"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-678027"],"event":{"end":"2022-12-11","place":"Abu Dhabi, ae","start":"2022-12-07","title":"2022 Findings of the Association for Computational Linguistics: EMNLP 2022","year":"2022"},"filters":{"added":"2023-07","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Asia, East","Europe, EU","OECD"],"country":["Italy","Japan"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","National Research Council of Italy (CNR)","RIKEN Center for Life Science Technologies","Scuola Normale Superiore di Pisa"],"level":"sci","mra":["soc"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"outlier dimensions that disrupt transformers are driven by frequency","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S01-000.00-678027","identifier":[{"id":"85144872662","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["ku-441127ed-5567-4b84-b20f-ab6a1b51f416"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Social Science"],"noi":["Local-1-1-CB7D61F6BEDCCAE22F884483E8C867A0"],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"it","key":"scuola normale superiore di pisa","loc":["Italy","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Scuola Normale Superiore di Pisa","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"it","key":"national research council of italy","loc":["Italy","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"National Research Council of Italy (CNR)","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"jp","key":"riken center for life science technologies","loc":["Japan","Asia, East","OECD"],"name":"RIKEN Center for Life Science Technologies","used":1},{"affno":"4","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Social Sciences, Faculty of Social Sciences, shared expenditure, Copenhagen Center for Social Data Science (SODAS)","Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Social Sciences, Faculty of Social Sciences, shared expenditure, Social Data Science"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1 2 3","first":"Giovanni","key":"puccettipau","last":"Puccetti","name":"Puccetti, Giovanni","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"4","first":"Anna","key":"rogerspau","last":"Rogers","locid":"e8d3de73-eb20-4bda-9f0e-6bf8a7bdac21","name":"Rogers, Anna","orcid":"0000-0002-4845-4023","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","first":"Aleksandr","key":"drozdpau","last":"Drozd","name":"Drozd, Aleksandr","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Felice","key":"dellorlettapau","last":"Dell'Orletta","name":"Dell'Orletta, Felice","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"status":"p"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-441127ed-5567-4b84-b20f-ab6a1b51f416"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Outlier Dimensions that Disrupt Transformers are Driven by Frequency","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-678027","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"Transformer-based language models are known to display anisotropic behavior: the token embeddings are not homogeneously spread in space, but rather accumulate along certain directions. A related recent finding is the outlier phenomenon: the parameters in the final element of Transformer layers that consistently have unusual magnitude in the same dimension across the model, and significantly degrade its performance if disabled. We replicate the evidence for the outlier phenomenon and we link it to the geometry of the embedding space. Our main finding is that in both BERT and RoBERTa the token frequency, known to contribute to anisotropicity, also contributes to the outlier phenomenon. In its turn, the outlier phenomenon contributes to the 'vertical' self-attention pattern that enables the model to focus on the special tokens. We also find that, surprisingly, the outlier effect on the model performance varies by layer, and that variance is also related to the correlation between outlier magnitude and encoded token frequency.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Puccetti, Giovanni","Rogers, Anna","Drozd, Aleksandr","Dell'Orletta, Felice"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-695260"],"filters":{"added":"2023-11","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Asia, East","Europe, EU","OECD"],"country":["Italy","Japan"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Italian Natural Language Processing Lab","RIKEN Center for Computational Science","Scuola Normale Superiore"],"keyword":["Anisotropic behavior","Outlier phenomenon","Self attention pattern","Token frequency","Transformer based language models"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"outliers dimensions that disrupt transformers are driven by frequency","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"https://aclanthology.org/2022.findings-emnlp.93/","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-695260","ids":["itu-82e28c07-cd2f-4074-ae41-7d2453b5fdea"],"keyword":["Anisotropic behavior","Outlier phenomenon","Self-attention pattern","Token frequency","Transformer-based language models"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-197396F8397AF9CD67763A204AC36785"],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"it","key":"scuola normale superiore","loc":["Italy","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Scuola Normale Superiore","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"it","key":"italian natural language processing lab","loc":["Italy","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Italian Natural Language Processing Lab","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"jp","key":"riken center for computational science","loc":["Japan","Asia, East","OECD"],"name":"RIKEN Center for Computational Science","used":1},{"affno":4,"key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU"}],"person":[{"affno":"1 2 3","corresponding":null,"first":"Giovanni","key":"puccettipau","last":"Puccetti","name":"Puccetti, Giovanni","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Anna","key":"rogerspau","last":"Rogers","locid":"c40e1e8a-50ee-4a7c-b7f0-2fc0fdd62adc","name":"Rogers, Anna","orcid":"0000-0002-4845-4023","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Aleksandr","key":"drozdpau","last":"Drozd","name":"Drozd, Aleksandr","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Felice","key":"dellorlettapau","last":"Dell'Orletta","name":"Dell'Orletta, Felice","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"publisher":"Association for Computational Linguistics","status":"p","title":"Findings of EMNLP 2022"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-82e28c07-cd2f-4074-ae41-7d2453b5fdea"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Outliers Dimensions that Disrupt Transformers Are Driven by Frequency","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-695260","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"Past efforts to synthesize and quantify the magnitude and change in carbon dioxide (CO2) fluxes in terrestrial ecosystems across the rapidly warming Arctic-boreal zone (ABZ) have provided valuable information but were limited in their geographical and temporal coverage. Furthermore, these efforts have been based on data aggregated over varying time periods, often with only minimal site ancillary data, thus limiting their potential to be used in large-scale carbon budget assessments. To bridge these gaps, we developed a standardized monthly database of Arctic-boreal CO2 fluxes (ABCflux) that aggregates in situ measurements of terrestrial net ecosystem CO2 exchange and its derived partitioned component fluxes: gross primary productivity and ecosystem respiration. The data span from 1989 to 2020 with over 70 supporting variables that describe key site conditions (e.g., vegetation and disturbance type), micrometeorological and environmental measurements (e.g., air and soil temperatures), and flux measurement techniques. Here, we describe these variables, the spatial and temporal distribution of observations, the main strengths and limitations of the database, and the potential research opportunities it enables. In total, ABCflux includes 244 sites and 6309 monthly observations; 136 sites and 2217 monthly observations represent tundra, and 108 sites and 4092 observations represent the boreal biome. The database includes fluxes estimated with chamber (19 % of the monthly observations), snow diffusion (3 %) and eddy covariance (78 %) techniques. The largest number of observations were collected during the climatological summer (June-August; 32 %), and fewer observations were available for autumn (September-October; 25 %), winter (December-February; 18 %), and spring (March-May; 25 %). ABCflux can be used in a wide array of empirical, remote sensing and modeling studies to improve understanding of the regional and temporal variability in CO2 fluxes and to better estimate the terrestrial ABZ CO2 budget. ABCflux is openly and freely available online (Virkkala et al., 2021b, https://doi.org/10.3334/ORNLDAAC/1934).","author_count":65,"contributors":["Virkkala, Anna-Maria","Natali, Susan M.","Rogers, Brendan M.","Watts, Jennifer D.","Savage, Kathleen","Connon, Sara June","Mauritz, Marguerite","Schuur, Edward A. G.","Peter, Darcy","Minions, Christina","Nojeim, Julia","Commane, Roisin","Emmerton, Craig A.","Goeckede, Mathias","Helbig, Manuel","Holl, David","Iwata, Hiroki","Kobayashi, Hideki","Kolari, Pasi","López-Blanco, Efrén","Marushchak, Maija E.","Mastepanov, Mikhail","Merbold, Lutz","Parmentier, Frans-Jan W.","Peichl, Matthias","Sachs, Torsten","Sonnentag, Oliver","Ueyama, Masahito","Voigt, Carolina","Aurela, Mika","Boike, Julia","Celis, Gerardo","Chae, Namyi","Christensen, Torben R.","Bret-Harte, M. Syndonia","Dengel, Sigrid","Dolman, Han","Edgar, Colin W.","Elberling, Bo","Euskirchen, Eugenie","Grelle, Achim","Hatakka, Juha","Humphreys, Elyn","Jarveoja, Jarvi","Kotani, Ayumi","Kutzbach, Lars","Laurila, Tuomas","Lohila, Annalea","Mammarella, Ivan","Matsuura, Yojiro","Meyer, Gesa","Nilsson, Mats B.","Oberbauer, Steven F.","Park, Sang-Jong","Petrov, Roman","Prokushkin, Anatoly S.","Schulze, Christopher","St Louis, Vincent L.","Tuittila, Eeva-Stiina","Tuovinen, Juha-Pekka","Quinton, William","Varlagin, Andrej","Zona, Donatella","Zyryanov, Viacheslav I."],"cuid":["S02-097.08-025044"],"filters":{"added":"2022-03","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"51-200 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"continent":["America, North","Asia, East","Europe, EU","Europe, Non-EU","Nordic","OECD"],"corresponding_author":["Virkkala, Anna-Maria"],"country":["Canada","Finland","Germany","Greenland","Japan","Netherlands","Norway","Russia","South Korea","Sweden","Switzerland","United States"],"glorg":["Agroscope","Alfred Wegener Institute - Helmholtz Centre for Polar and Marine Research","All Global Affiliations","Carleton University"