{"abstract":"A myriad of explainability methods have been proposed in recent years, but there is little consensus on how to evaluate them. While automatic metrics allow for quick enchmarking, it isn’t clear how such metrics reflect human interaction with explanations. Human evaluation is of paramount importance, but previous protocols fail to account for belief biases affecting human performance, which may lead to misleading conclusions. We provide an overview of belief bias, its role in human evaluation, and ideas for NLP practitioners on how to account for it. For t o experimental paradigms, we present a case study of gradientbased explainability ntroducing simple ways to account for humans’ prior beliefs: models of varying quality and adversarial examples. We show that conclusions about the highest performing methods change when introducing such controls, pointing to the importance of accounting for belief bias in evaluation. 1 Int","author_count":3,"contributors":["Gonzalez, Ana Valeria","Rogers, Anna","Søgaard, Anders"],"cuid":["S02-090.36-692212"],"event":{"acronym":"ACL","end":"2021-08-06","place":"Virtual, Online","start":"2021-08-01","title":"Joint Conference of the 59th Annual Meeting of the Association for Computational Linguistics and the 11th International Joint Conference on Natural Language Processing, ACL-IJCNLP 2021","year":"2021"},"filters":{"added":"2022-03","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"keyword":["Belief Bias","Evaluation metrics","Explainability Methods","Gradient Based Explainability","Human Evaluation"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"on the interaction of belief bias and explanations","src":["itu","ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU","DI-U-KU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.18653/v1/2021.findings-acl.259","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S02-090.36-692212","identifier":[{"id":"85112110625","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-ce1e3af2-0019-4d8a-be11-bd55fad9412f","ku-88e121bd-d625-4a92-b4f0-db150f0e8d02"],"keyword":["Belief Bias","Evaluation Metrics","Explainability Methods","Gradient-Based Explainability","Human Evaluation"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-18E1F2F85AE48AC9BB7C5C3BFCEE30A4"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.18653/v1/2021.findings-acl.259","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Science, Department of Computer Science, Machine Learning","Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Science, Department of Computer Science, Natural Language Processing"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","first":"Ana Valeria","key":"gonzálezpau","last":"González","locid":"15bca55a-7863-4892-ae02-56f2d27225e7","name":"Gonzalez, Ana Valeria","name_org":"González, Ana Valeria","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Anna","key":"rogerspau","last":"Rogers","locid":"e8d3de73-eb20-4bda-9f0e-6bf8a7bdac21","name":"Rogers, Anna","orcid":"0000-0002-4845-4023","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Anders","key":"søgaardpau","last":"Søgaard","locid":"5cfea506-104a-4b40-b63b-e29d037159b0","name":"Søgaard, Anders","orcid":"0000-0001-5250-4276","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.18653/v1/2021.findings-acl.259","pages":"2930-2942","place":"Online","publisher":"Association for Computational Linguistics","status":"p","title":"Findings of the Association for Computational Linguistics: ACL-IJCNLP 2021"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85112110625"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-ce1e3af2-0019-4d8a-be11-bd55fad9412f","ku-88e121bd-d625-4a92-b4f0-db150f0e8d02"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen","KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"On the Interaction of Belief Bias and Explanations","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-090.36-692212","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"Much of recent progress in NLU was shown to be due to models' learning dataset-specific heuristics. We conduct a case study of generalization in NLI (from MNLI to the adversarially constructed HANS dataset) in a range of BERT-based architectures (adapters, Siamese Transformers, HEX debiasing), as well as with subsampling the data and increasing the model size. We report 2 successful and 3 unsuccessful strategies, all providing insights into how Transformer-based models learn to generalize.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Bhargava, Prajjwal","Drozd, Aleksandr","Rogers, Anna"],"cuid":["S02-100.00-692203"],"filters":{"added":"2022-04","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"continent":["America, North","Asia, East","OECD"],"country":["Japan","United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","RIKEN Center for Computational Science","Tokyo Institute of Technology","University of Texas"],"keyword":["Adversarial robustness","BERT based architectures","Generalization","Natural Language Understanding","Transformer models"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci","soc"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"generalization in nli ways not to go beyond simple heuristics","src":["itu","ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU","DI-U-KU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"https://aclanthology.org/2021.insights-1.18","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S02-100.00-692203","ids":["itu-40cdd835-0fe9-47eb-a133-d72a41aacf01","ku-222844cd-f1d0-41a5-96ec-723837a773d8"],"keyword":["Adversarial robustness","BERT-based architectures","Generalization","Natural Language Understanding","Transformer models","t/generalization","task/NLI"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology","Social Science"],"noi":["Local-1-2-619C0AFBF6D1E19AA289A0B75D841A8D"],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"us","key":"university of texas","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"University of Texas","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"jp","key":"riken center for computational science","loc":["Japan","Asia, East","OECD"],"name":"RIKEN Center for Computational Science","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"jp","key":"tokyo institute of technology","loc":["Japan","Asia, East","OECD"],"name":"Tokyo Institute of Technology","used":1},{"affno":"4","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Social Sciences, Faculty of Social Sciences, shared expenditure, Social Data Science"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1},{"affno":5,"key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU"}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Prajjwal","key":"bhargavapau","last":"Bhargava","name":"Bhargava, Prajjwal","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2 3","corresponding":null,"first":"Aleksandr","key":"drozdpau","last":"Drozd","name":"Drozd, Aleksandr","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"4","first":"Anna","key":"rogerspau","last":"Rogers","locid":"e8d3de73-eb20-4bda-9f0e-6bf8a7bdac21","name":"Rogers, Anna","orcid":"0000-0002-4845-4023","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"pages":"125-135","place":"Online and Punta Cana, Dominican Republic","publisher":"Association for Computational Linguistics (ACL)","status":"p","title":"Proceedings of the Second Workshop on Insights from Negative Results in NLP"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","ITU IT University of Copenhagen","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-40cdd835-0fe9-47eb-a133-d72a41aacf01","ku-222844cd-f1d0-41a5-96ec-723837a773d8"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen","KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Generalization in NLI: Ways (Not) To Go Beyond Simple Heuristics","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-100.00-692203","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"In 2008 we published the first set of guidelines for standardizing research in autophagy. Since then, research on this topic has continued to accelerate, and many new scientists have entered the field. Our knowledge base and relevant new technologies have also been expanding. Accordingly, it is important to update these guidelines formonitoring autophagy in different organisms. Various reviews have described the range of assays that have been used for this purpose. Nevertheless, there continues to be confusion regarding acceptable methods to measure autophagy, especially in multicellular eukaryotes. A key point that needs to be emphasized is that there is a Do not distribute. difference between measurements that monitor the numbers or volume of autophagic elements (e.g., autophagosomes or autolysosomes) at any stage of the autophagic process vs. those that measure flux through the autophagy pathway (i.e., the complete process); thus, a block in macroautophagy that results in autophagosome accumulation needs to be differentiated fromstimuli that result in increased autophagic activity, defined as increased autophagy induction coupled with increased delivery to, and degradation within, lysosomes (in most higher eukaryotes and some protists such as Dictyostelium) or the vacuole (in plants and fungi). In other words, it is especially important that investigators new to the field understand that the appearance of more autophagosomes does not necessarily equate withmore autophagy. In fact, in many cases, autophagosomes accumulate because of a block in trafficking to lysosomes without a concomitant change in autophagosome biogenesis,whereas an increase in autolysosomes may reflect a reduction in degradative activity. Here, we present a set of guidelines for the selection and interpretation of methods for use by investigators who aim to examine macroautophagy and related processes, as well as for reviewers who need to provide realistic and reasonable critiques of papers that are focused on these processes. These guidelines are not meant to be a formulaic set of rules, because the appropriate assays depend in part on the question being asked and the system being used. In addition, we emphasize that no individual assay is guaranteed to be themost appropriate one in every situation, and we strongly recommend the use of multiple assays to monitor autophagy. In these guidelines, we consider these various methods of assessing autophagy and what information can, or cannot, be obtained from them. Finally, by discussing the merits and limits of particular autophagy assays, we hope to encourage technical innovation in the field.","author_count":2922,"contributors":["Klionsky, Daniel J","Abdel-Aziz, Amal Kamal","Abdelfatah, Sara","Abdellatif, Mahmoud","Abdoli, Asghar","Abel, Steffen","Abeliovich, Hagai","Abildgaard, Marie H","Abudu, Yakubu Princely","Acevedo-Arozena, Abraham","Adamopoulos, Iannis E","Adeli, Khosrow","Adolph, Timon E","Adornetto, Annagrazia","Aflaki, Elma","Agam, Galila","Agarwal, Anupam","Aggarwal, Bharat B","Agnello, Maria","Agostinis, Patrizia","Agrewala, Javed N","Agrotis, Alexander","Aguilar, Patricia V","Ahmad, S Tariq","Ahmed, Zubair M","Ahumada-Castro, Ulises","Aits, Sonja","Aizawa, Shu","Akkoc, Yunus","Akoumianaki, Tonia","Akpinar, Hafize Aysin","Al-Abd, Ahmed M","Al-Akra, Lina","Al-Gharaibeh, Abeer","Alaoui-Jamali, Moulay A","Alberti, Simon","Alcocer-Gómez, Elísabet","Alessandri, Cristiano","Ali, Muhammad","Alim Al-Bari, M Abdul","Aliwaini, Saeb","Alizadeh, Javad","Almacellas, Eugènia","Almasan, Alexandru","Alonso, Alicia","Alonso, Guillermo D","Altan-Bonnet, Nihal","Altieri, Dario C","Álvarez, Élida M C","Alves, Sara","Alves da Costa, Cristine","Alzaharna, Mazen M","Amadio, Marialaura","Amantini, Consuelo","Amaral, Cristina","Ambrosio, Susanna","Amer, Amal O","Ammanathan, Veena","An, Zhenyi","Andersen, Stig U","Andrabi, Shaida A","Andrade-Silva, Magaiver","Andres, Allen M","Angelini, Sabrina","Ann, David",